= Lost-in-the-middle effect =

== Overview ==
The '''lost-in-the-middle effect''' is reduced ability of a language model to use relevant information when that information appears in the middle of a long input compared with favorable positions near the beginning or end.

== Positional performance ==
Long-context performance can follow a U-shaped curve: information near the beginning and end is used more reliably than information in intermediate positions.

== Relation to serial position ==
The behavioral shape resembles human [[Primacy effect]] and [[Recency effect]], but this resemblance does not establish a shared mechanism.

== Evaluation ==
A controlled test moves the same relevant information across positions while holding content approximately constant.

== References ==
* Nelson F. Liu et al., “Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts”, ''Transactions of the Association for Computational Linguistics'' 12 (2024): 157–173.

